## I. THE RECORD, IN HUMAN AND CHRONOLOGICAL TERMS

This action began with a wheelchair fall on a bus on October 8, 2019. The claim did not begin as a story manufactured for litigation: a physician's October 7, 2019 statement documented Rodriguez's preexisting medical vulnerability before the occurrence, and an emergency department record dated October 8 described how the bus stopped abruptly, Rodriguez fell forward from her wheelchair, and her mother also fell. The hospital records reported contusions, imaging and a missed medical appointment. **[DOCUMENT: proposed Exhibits A–B]** The circumstances and liability remain matters for adjudication; the importance here is that two separate individuals entered litigation with distinct claims.

On September 21, 2020, the complaint placed both claims before this Court. Alcaraz appeared in her own right; Rodriguez's claim was pursued through the guardian-ad-litem framework. Their claims could arise from one incident without becoming the same legal interest. The relief sought years later must therefore ask separately what happened to each claim. **[DOCUMENT: complaint and guardian-ad-litem records; Exhibit D]**

The decisive interval began on **April 22, 2024**. The records identify Rodriguez's hospital admission on that date. Separately, counsel initiated withdrawal proceedings for the two plaintiffs that evening, at **8:47 p.m.** and **9:08 p.m.**, respectively, citing irreconcilable differences. The events share a date; the supplied material does not establish that any lawyer knew the hour-by-hour clinical course. The pending withdrawal proceedings were scheduled against a jury-trial setting of **May 31, 2024**. **[DOCUMENT: withdrawal filings; later-authored hospital records; Exhibits H–I]**

