The materials most directly relevant to the threshold issues are: **both physical originals; all pages of each release; the native May 1 email with attachments and complete message headers; original scanner and PDF files; any approval, settlement-instruction and disbursement communications; the April 22 withdrawal motions and orders; the May 9 notice and proof of service; trial-vacation minute orders; Rodriguez's guardian ad litem appointment and any compromise petition/order; death and successor papers; the December dismissal request; and the full payment and trust ledger.** The requested preservation should extend to material originals and existing native metadata, without creating or backdating information.

Inspection and compulsory production should proceed by an available subpoena, discovery request, or specific court order after notice and consideration of privileges, confidentiality and proportionality. A narrowly tailored evidentiary hearing need not become an unrestricted inquiry into all records in the action.

## XV. REQUESTED FINDINGS AND RELIEF

Alcaraz asks the Court to make **separate findings** concerning her individual claim and Rodriguez's surviving claim; whether an agreement was formed for each; who possessed authority; whether section 372 approval was required and supplied; what post-death capacity existed; whether ratification occurred; and which dismissal components, if any, should be vacated. If the Court determines that the existing record does not permit ultimate relief, it should, to the extent procedurally authorized, permit an orderly evidentiary hearing and focused evidence preservation before resolving disputed factual predicates.

If relief is granted, the Court should restore the affected claim or claims to the active docket, set a case management conference, and provide for formal establishment of proper capacity for any surviving Rodriguez claim. No person should be designated successor or estate representative solely by virtue of this motion without compliance with the governing statute.

## XVI. CONCLUSION

Alcaraz acknowledges that documents were signed, that counsel reported a settlement, that the Court vacated scheduled proceedings, and that dismissal with prejudice was entered. Her challenge is **not to the historical existence of those events**. Her challenge is to the **authority linking them**. The Court should determine whether her own claim was relinquished through effective consent, whether Rodriguez's separate protected interests were validly compromised and approved, and whether the subsequent actions culminating in dismissal rested on proper authority. Only after those questions are resolved should the recorded disposition be treated as conclusively determining both plaintiffs' rights.

